REFUSED: --control 256 at BLOCK_SIZE_N=256 cannot be held by one thread block: the 256x256 fp32 accumulator is 65536 32-bit registers per thread block against a per-block file of 65536, so the accumulator ALONE is 100% of the file and nothing is left for the pointers, the indices or the K loop. No num_warps fixes this: warps divide the total across more threads, they do not shrink it, and num_stages and BLOCK_SIZE_K do not touch it at all. The kernel spills to local memory and its time is not the time of the tiling this sweep is about. THE ARM AS DESIGNED CANNOT RUN AT THIS GEOMETRY, and no pin rescues it; the search and the alternatives are printed below.
CONTROL SEARCH at BLOCK_SIZE_N=256, BLOCK_SIZE_K=64, 2 B per element, sm_90
  the bills: accumulator BLOCK_M x BLOCK_SIZE_N fp32 registers per block against 65536 and 255 per thread; shared memory num_stages x (BLOCK_M x BLOCK_K + BLOCK_K x BLOCK_N) x 2 B against 227 KiB
  the subject BLOCK_M=128 caps at 147.4 Op/B = 0.96x the ridge, at alpha 0.558 in both slots of moe.bench.ai_model.cap. A control has to sit ABOVE that line and be held by one block
  BLOCK_M=256: cap 217.2 Op/B = 1.42x the ridge (+69.8 Op/B on the subject, a predicted separation of +0.035 of the roof); accumulator 256x256 = 65536 registers per block, 100% of the file
      REFUSED AT EVERY WARP AND STAGE COUNT: 8 warps = 256 reg/thread, 16 warps = 128 reg/thread, 32 warps = 64 reg/thread, and 65536 of 65536 per block in all of them. Warps divide this total, they do not shrink it; stages and BLOCK_SIZE_K do not touch it.
  BLOCK_M=512: cap 284.6 Op/B = 1.86x the ridge (+137.2 Op/B on the subject, a predicted separation of +0.035 of the roof); accumulator 512x256 = 131072 registers per block, 200% of the file
      REFUSED AT EVERY WARP AND STAGE COUNT: 8 warps = 512 reg/thread, 16 warps = 256 reg/thread, 32 warps = 128 reg/thread, and 131072 of 65536 per block in all of them. Warps divide this total, they do not shrink it; stages and BLOCK_SIZE_K do not touch it.
  BLOCK_M=1024: cap 336.8 Op/B = 2.20x the ridge (+189.4 Op/B on the subject, a predicted separation of +0.035 of the roof); accumulator 1024x256 = 262144 registers per block, 400% of the file
      REFUSED AT EVERY WARP AND STAGE COUNT: 8 warps = 1024 reg/thread, 16 warps = 512 reg/thread, 32 warps = 256 reg/thread, and 262144 of 65536 per block in all of them. Warps divide this total, they do not shrink it; stages and BLOCK_SIZE_K do not touch it.
  NO TILE ABOVE BLOCK_M=128 CAN BE PINNED AT BLOCK_SIZE_N=256.
  AND NO ARM OF THIS EXPERIMENT REACHES 'CEILING BINDING AT THE PRODUCTION TILE' ON THIS CARD, AT ANY BLOCK_SIZE_N. Over 56 power-of-two tiles up to 2048x1024, 17 clear the ridge of 152.81 Op/B at alpha 0.558 and the smallest accumulator among them is 65536 registers, against a per-block file of 65536. So no tile a thread block can hold is capable of reaching a compute roof here, C4 fails for every control that can be built, and the best verdict any pairing can reach is 'TILE-DEPENDENT GAP, CEILING UNLOCATED'. The two limits very nearly coincide, which is the finding: the register file runs out exactly where the arithmetic intensity would have become enough.
THE STUDY CANNOT CONFIRM ITS HEADLINE AT vLLM'S SHIPPED CONFIGURATION ON sm_90. At a fixed BLOCK_SIZE_N the only way to buy a tile more headroom is a larger BLOCK_SIZE_M, the only power of two above 128 that Triton will pin is 256, and a 256x256 fp32 accumulator is 65536 32-bit registers: the entire per-block register file of every architecture from sm_70 to sm_100. No num_warps, no num_stages and no BLOCK_SIZE_K changes that total. So at BLOCK_SIZE_N=256 there is no positive control, and the shortfall a subject-only run measures has nothing to be attributed against. This half needs no --capability: the register file is the same on every card this study can reach.
WHAT WOULD CONFIRM IT, in the order of how much each concedes:
  1. A card whose per-block register file exceeds 256 KiB. No NVIDIA architecture this study can reach has one; the file has been 64 K registers per SM and per block since sm_70.
  2. A kernel whose accumulator is not resident at full BM x BN -- a split-N or a two-pass formulation. vLLM's fused_moe kernel is not one, so such a run would measure a different kernel and could not be quoted about the one production runs.
  3. The subject at BLOCK_SIZE_N=128, where a BLOCK_M=256 control fits at 8 warps and 4 stages (128 registers per thread, 192 KiB of shared memory) and does have headroom on the subject there: 147.4 Op/B against 111.6, a predicted separation of +0.235 of the roof. IT STILL DOES NOT CONFIRM THE HEADLINE: 147.4 is 0.96x the ridge, so that control is memory bound by construction, C4 fails before the run and the arm lands 'TILE-DEPENDENT GAP, CEILING UNLOCATED'. And CHECKED IN THE SHIPPED FILES, NOT ASSUMED: moe/bench/hardware/vllm_configs holds no BLOCK_SIZE_M=128 entry at BLOCK_SIZE_N=128 for either model this study measures, on either dtype -- every 128 entry on the H200 ships BLOCK_SIZE_N=256. So that arm buys a GAP at a configuration nobody ships, and it may not be quoted as the production claim.
  4. A control at BLOCK_M=256 with a BLOCK_SIZE_N of its OWN, 128, against this arm's subject at 256. REFUSED, and for a reason that is NOT item 3's: moe.bench.ai_model.cap is 2 / (b (alpha_b/BM + alpha_a/BN + 1/K)), SYMMETRIC in the two tile dimensions, so 256x128 has EXACTLY this subject's cap -- 147.42 Op/B against 147.42 on mixtral-8x7b at alpha 0.558 in both slots -- along with the same accumulator, the same shared memory and the same residency. Its headroom is zero, so C3's difference is zero by construction; item 3 changes the SUBJECT too and does buy headroom. block_m_crossing_sweep.ai_cap would have called this one 2x the headroom, because that form is 2 BM / (alpha b) and BLOCK_SIZE_N does not appear in it.
  5. `--control none`: the subject alone, which can REFUTE this claim and can never confirm it. That is the only one of the five this session can buy at BLOCK_SIZE_N=256, and what it buys is stated where it is offered.
NONE OF THE FIVE CONFIRMS THE HEADLINE ON THIS CARD, AND NO BLOCK_SIZE_N DOES EITHER. Items 1 and 2 need hardware or a kernel this study cannot reach, item 3 buys a gap and not a ceiling, item 4 buys nothing, and item 5 concedes the question. The reachability line in the CONTROL SEARCH above is why the list has no sixth entry: of the 17 power-of-two tiles whose cap clears this card's ridge, the smallest accumulator is 65536 registers against a per-block file of 65536, so C4 is pre-registered to FAIL for every control this hardware can hold. The registered outcome of this experiment on sm_90 is a fraction of the roof, with or without a control.
